# November 18, 2026: Frederick County, Maryland (with Washington Monument just over the line in Washington County),
# region md, drawn at random by tools/choose.py. First real pass in Maryland (only Daniels was on the map).
# Facts come from the linked sources, opened in this session: Wikipedia read as raw text (wiki_raw.py), Maryland DNR
# park pages, National Park Service pages, an HMdb marker and the Frederick County Landmarks Foundation's visit page,
# all read through tools/page.py. Where a managing body's page could not be opened the access note says
# "hours not checked". Brick values come from the sources' own words about what the place is built of, or from a
# picture opened with tools/brick.py look ('Catoctin Furnace.jpg': stone stack with a red brick arch; 'Capitol Limited at
# Point of Rocks.jpg': red brick station; 'St Pauls Episcopal Church Point of Rocks.jpg': red brick; 'Rose Hill Manor MD1.jpg':
# brick painted white). '?' where neither settled it (the Gathland arch picture shows stone with red panels and an arch ring
# that could be brick or brownstone).
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'md_vdxv','label':'Frederick County, Maryland: furnace, covered bridges, canal and battlefield','added':'2026-11-18 08:56'}
DNR='https://dnr.maryland.gov/publiclands/Pages/western/'
PLACES=[
# ---- Catoctin Mountain and the north county ----
N('catoctin-furnace-isabella','Catoctin Furnace (Isabella stack)','Catoctin Furnace','md','industry','ruins',39.58131,-77.43467,'site','public','walk','','1774–1903',
  'An ironworks at the foot of Catoctin Mountain begun by the four Johnson brothers in the 1770s; DNR says it was in blast by 1776 and made ammunition for the Continental Army, including shells fired at Yorktown. Enslaved Africans made up much of the early workforce. A second stack, "Isabella", was added in the 1850s (DNR says 1857; Wikipedia gives dates from 1853 to 1867) and ran until 1893; a third, coal-fired stack of 1873 ("Deborah") was taken down two years after the works closed in 1903.',
  'The Isabella stack still stands, the focal point of the remains, with a reconstructed 1858 casting shed beside it. A short mid-1800s tramway bed runs north toward the old ore banks, and an interpretive trail leads past stone and log workers\' houses on MD 806 to the Catoctin Furnace Historical Society\'s Museum of the Ironworker and the African American cemetery trail (DNR). Most village houses are private homes: keep to the road and trail.',
  'Cannonballs for Yorktown, made by enslaved ironworkers.',
  accn='Cunningham Falls State Park, Manor Area (Maryland DNR), off US 15 three miles south of Thurmont. Park hours on the DNR page: 8 a.m. to sunset April to October, 10 a.m. to sunset November to March. The park lists day-use fees per vehicle; whether the furnace lot charges was not checked. The historical society\'s museum and Collier\'s Log House open by appointment and at events (443-463-6437).',
  links=[W('Catoctin Furnace'),['Maryland DNR: Catoctin Furnace',DNR+'CunninghamFalls/Catoctin-Furnace.aspx'],['Maryland DNR: Cunningham Falls State Park',DNR+'Cunningham.aspx']],conf='mixed',
  th=[39.58133,-77.43399],thn='African American Cemetery Trail lot, Catoctin Furnace',brick='some'),
N('camp-misty-mount','Camp Misty Mount WPA cabin camp','Thurmont','md','civic','standing',39.64194,-77.44861,'site','public','walk','','1935–1938',
  'The first of three cabin camps built by the Works Progress Administration for the Catoctin Mountain Recreational Demonstration Area, on worn-out farm and charcoal land the federal government bought in the 1930s. The NPS says it was finished in spring 1937 (Wikipedia gives 1935 to 1938 for the buildings) and leased to the Salvation Army from 1938. Camp 3 of the same programme became Camp David.',
  'About 35 rustic log buildings, sleeping cabins, lodges and service buildings, in a simplified National Park Service Rustic style; the NPS says they were built of local American chestnut, whose slow decay explains why they have lasted. The camp is still rented out as a cabin campground, so the cabins themselves are for guests; interiors not checked.',
  'Hand-built chestnut cabins from the camp that begat Camp David.',
  accn='Catoctin Mountain Park (National Park Service). The park is open daily, dawn to dusk; the visitor center 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. (NPS hours page). Cabins are booked through Recreation.gov; season not checked. Park at the Blue Blazes Whiskey Still trail lot or the visitor center.',
  links=[W('Camp Misty Mount Historic District'),['NPS: Historic Camp Misty Mount','https://www.nps.gov/cato/learn/historyculture/historic-camp-misty-mount.htm'],['NPS: Catoctin Mountain Park hours','https://www.nps.gov/cato/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],conf='mixed',
  th=[39.64282,-77.44665],thn='Blue Blazes Whiskey Still trail lot',brick='no'),
N('roddy-road-covered-bridge','Roddy Road Covered Bridge','Thurmont','md','landmark','standing',39.64092,-77.39370,'site','public','drive','','1850s',
  'A small one-lane king post wooden covered bridge over Owens Creek north of Thurmont, 40 feet long. Wikipedia dates it between 1850 and 1860; the HMdb marker in Thurmont says 1856. It is the smallest of the three covered bridges left in northern Frederick County, out of about a hundred that Maryland has had.',
  'The single wooden king post span on Roddy Creek Road, just off US 15. Listed on the National Register in 1978.',
  'The smallest covered bridge in the county.',
  accn='On a county road; seen from the roadside. Whether it still carries cars, and where to pull off, was not checked: do not park on the bridge approaches.',
  links=[W('Roddy Road Covered Bridge'),['HMdb: Thurmont marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=131818']],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('loys-station-covered-bridge','Loys Station Covered Bridge','Thurmont','md','landmark','standing',39.60861,-77.35139,'exact','public','walk','','1848; rebuilt 1994',
  'A multiple king post wooden covered bridge over Owens Creek, named for a Western Maryland Railroad stop at Loys. Wikipedia says it was built in 1848; the HMdb marker says about 1880. A concrete pier and steel beams were put in about 1929–30. An arsonist burned it in 1991 to collect insurance; it was rebuilt from the surviving framing to its 1930 form and reopened on July 4, 1994.',
  'The 90-foot bridge, now on a middle pier, carrying Old Frederick Road through a small county park. The marker says original timbers remain throughout.',
  'Burned for the insurance, rebuilt from its own bones.',
  accn='Loy\'s Station Park, 3600 Old Frederick Road (Frederick County Parks and Recreation map). Small lot by the bridge. Park hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Loys Station Covered Bridge'),['HMdb: Thurmont marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=131818'],['Frederick County: Loy\'s Station Park map','https://www.recreater.com/DocumentCenter/View/312']],conf='mixed',
  th=[39.60817,-77.35212],thn='Loy\'s Station Park lot, Old Frederick Road',brick='no'),
N('utica-covered-bridge','Utica Covered Bridge','Lewistown','md','landmark','standing',39.5252,-77.3976,'exact','public','drive','','1834; moved 1891',
  'A Burr truss wooden covered bridge first built in 1834 across the Monocacy River at the Devilbiss crossing. A flood in 1889 damaged it, and the half that survived was moved to Fishing Creek in 1891. Like Loys Station, it was later given a middle pier.',
  'The surviving half-bridge, a Burr arch truss on a centre pier, over Fishing Creek on Utica Road. Listed on the National Register in 1978.',
  'Half a Monocacy River bridge, moved to a creek after the 1889 flood.',
  accn='On a county road; seen from the roadside. Whether it carries cars was not checked. Utica Park (county) has a lot on Utica Road about 500 m south-west.',
  links=[W('Utica Covered Bridge')],
  th=[39.52211,-77.40167],thn='Utica Park lot, Utica Road',brick='no'),
N('graceham-moravian-church','Graceham Moravian Church and parsonage','Graceham','md','civic','standing',39.61538,-77.37887,'exact','view','drive','','1797; church 1822',
  'The Moravian congregation here was organised in 1758 as the "Manakosy" (Monocacy) church; its first congregation house went up in 1749 and the village took the name Graceham in 1785. The meeting house and parsonage were built in 1797 and the present church added to them in 1822. With its God\'s Acre of uniform flat gravestones it is Maryland\'s only remaining 18th-century Moravian settlement.',
  'A two-storey Flemish bond brick church, covered in white stucco because the brick had deteriorated, joined to the 1797 parsonage, with the flat-stone God\'s Acre beside it. Interior not checked.',
  'Maryland\'s last Moravian settlement and its flat-stone graveyard.',
  accn='An active church at 8231 Rocky Ridge Road (MD 77). Seen from the road; visiting hours not checked. The church grounds are private: ask before parking or walking the graveyard.',
  links=[W('Graceham Moravian Church and Parsonage')],brick='no'),
# ---- Frederick ----
N('schifferstadt-frederick','Schifferstadt','Frederick','md','civic','standing',39.42369,-77.42698,'exact','public','drive','','1758',
  'A German colonial stone farmhouse completed in 1758 by the Brunner family, German immigrants who settled the land in 1736 and named it after Klein Schifferstadt in the Rhineland-Palatinate. It is the oldest standing house in Frederick and was made a National Historic Landmark in 2016. The Frederick County Landmarks Foundation keeps it as an architectural museum.',
  'The original stone house. Wikipedia says it was built with two great hearth fireplaces and five-plate cast-iron stoves dated 1758; one stove survives. Enter through the gift shop to the left of the stone house.',
  'The oldest house in Frederick, with its 1758 iron stove.',
  accn='Schifferstadt Architectural Museum, 1110 Rosemont Avenue (Frederick County Landmarks Foundation). Open Saturdays and Sundays 2 to 5 p.m., weekdays by appointment (301-663-3885), per the foundation\'s visit page; Wikipedia says weekends April to December. Free parking along West 2nd Street or behind the church next door.',
  links=[W('Schifferstadt (Frederick, Maryland)'),['Frederick County Landmarks Foundation: Visit','https://www.fredericklandmarks.org/visit-us']],brick='no'),
N('hessian-barracks-frederick','Hessian Barracks','Frederick','md','fort','standing',39.40903,-77.40939,'exact','view','walk','','1777–1781',
  'Barracks the State of Maryland contracted for in 1777 and finished in 1781, used to hold British and German prisoners of war in the Revolution. In the 1800s they served as a state armory, a silkworm house and, after South Mountain and Antietam in 1862, a military hospital. Since 1867 the site has been the Maryland School for the Deaf; the western of the two L-shaped buildings was pulled down in 1871.',
  'The surviving two-storey stone barracks with gallery porches and a gable roof, on the school\'s campus. Interior not checked.',
  'Where Hessian prisoners waited out the Revolution.',
  accn='On the campus of the Maryland School for the Deaf, a working school. View from the street; ask the school before going onto the grounds. Hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Hessian Barracks')],brick='no'),
N('rose-hill-manor-frederick','Rose Hill Manor','Frederick','md','civic','standing',39.43639,-77.40583,'exact','public','drive','','c.1790–1798',
  'The retirement home of Thomas Johnson, Maryland\'s first elected governor and a Supreme Court justice, built in the mid-1790s by his daughter and son-in-law; Wikipedia says work ran eight years from the foundation to occupation about 1798. The land was part of the Tasker\'s Chance tract, settled by German farmers. Frederick County keeps it as a park with a children\'s museum.',
  'A brick house, painted white, on a low fieldstone foundation with brick cellar walls, a two-storey pedimented portico with Doric columns below and Ionic above, a chimney at each gable end, and a kitchen wing with a fireplace and beehive oven (Wikipedia). What original fabric can be seen inside on a tour was not checked.',
  'A first governor\'s retirement house.',
  accn='Rose Hill Manor Park & Museums (Frederick County Parks and Recreation), off Thomas Johnson Drive. The county\'s page could not be opened here: hours, fees and tour times not checked.',
  links=[W('Rose Hill Manor'),['Frederick County: Rose Hill Manor Park','https://www.recreater.com/264/Rose-Hill-Manor-Park']],brick='no'),
# ---- Monocacy National Battlefield ----
N('thomas-farm-monocacy','Thomas Farm (Araby), Monocacy battlefield','Frederick','md','civic','standing',39.35890,-77.39152,'site','public','drive','','c.1780',
  'A farm whose brick mansion the NPS dates to about 1780 and a Scottish merchant, James Marshall; it was called Araby from 1831 and bought in 1860 by Christian Keefer Thomas. On July 9, 1864 the farm saw some of the heaviest fighting of the Battle of Monocacy, changing hands several times while the family sheltered in the cellar; in August 1864 Grant met Sheridan in the house. The NPS bought the farm in 2001.',
  'The two-storey brick mansion, now the park headquarters, and its outbuildings. The tenant house is open 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. with exhibits on the whole park (NPS itinerary page). The Thomas Farm loop trail looks across to the Worthington Farm.',
  'Grant planned the Shenandoah campaign in this parlour.',
  accn='Monocacy National Battlefield (National Park Service), a stop on the park\'s driving tour. Grounds open daily 7:30 a.m. to 20 minutes after sunset (NPS hours page). The mansion is offices; only the tenant house is open.',
  links=[W('Monocacy National Battlefield'),['NPS: Thomas Farm','https://www.nps.gov/mono/learn/historyculture/thomas_farm.htm'],['NPS: one-day itinerary','https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/plan-like-a-park-ranger-1-day-itinerary-monocacy-national-battlefield.htm']],brick='?'),
N('gambrill-mill-monocacy','Gambrill Mill','Frederick','md','industry','standing',39.36717,-77.38695,'site','public','walk','','1830',
  'A three-storey merchant flour mill, also called Araby Mill, built in 1830 and bought by James H. Gambrill in 1855; a sawmill, a plaster mill and a miller\'s house stood with it. During the Battle of Monocacy the Union used it as a field hospital before Confederates took it at the end of the day. In the 1920s the top storey came off and the race was partly filled when it was made a house.',
  'The cut-down mill, now NPS staff offices, and the half-mile Gambrill Mill trail with waysides and a wheelchair-accessible boardwalk along the Monocacy River (NPS). The building is not open.',
  'A battle hospital with its top floor sawn off.',
  accn='Monocacy National Battlefield (National Park Service), lot on the Gambrill Mill trail off Urbana Pike. Grounds open daily 7:30 a.m. to 20 minutes after sunset.',
  links=[['NPS: Gambrill Mill','https://www.nps.gov/mono/learn/historyculture/gambrill_mill.htm'],['NPS: hours','https://www.nps.gov/mono/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  th=[39.36721,-77.38742],thn='Gambrill Mill trail lot',brick='?'),
# ---- The Potomac side ----
N('monocacy-aqueduct','Monocacy Aqueduct','Dickerson','md','canal','standing',39.22361,-77.45194,'exact','public','walk','canal','1829–1833',
  'The largest aqueduct on the Chesapeake and Ohio Canal, carrying it over the Monocacy River just above the Potomac. Built from 1829 to 1833 to Benjamin Wright\'s plans, mostly of quartzite from the foot of Sugarloaf Mountain. In September 1862 the lock keeper talked Confederates under D. H. Hill out of blowing it up; a later party could not drill deep enough to breach it. Steel bands went on after the floods of the 1970s, and the NPS finished a restoration in 2004.',
  'Seven stone arches on six piers. Its length is given as 438 feet by Wikipedia and 516 feet by the NPS, and Wikipedia calls it Aqueduct No. 2 while the NPS calls it the third of twelve. The ruins of the old Monocacy village granary, which had bays for boats, are beside the parking lot.',
  'The canal\'s biggest water bridge, saved by a lock keeper\'s argument.',
  accn='Chesapeake and Ohio Canal National Historical Park (National Park Service). Small lot off Mouth of Monocacy Road with the towpath beside it (C&O Canal Trust). Park hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Monocacy Aqueduct'),['NPS: Monocacy Aqueduct','https://www.nps.gov/places/monocacy-aqueduct.htm'],['C&O Canal Trust: Monocacy Aqueduct parking','https://www.canaltrust.org/pyv/monocacy-aqueduct-parking/']],conf='mixed',
  th=[39.22442,-77.44957],thn='Monocacy Aqueduct lot, Mouth of Monocacy Road',brick='no'),
N('point-of-rocks-station','Point of Rocks B&O station','Point of Rocks','md','rail','standing',39.27353,-77.53333,'exact','view','drive','stations','1873',
  'A Gothic Revival station built by the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad in 1873 to E. Francis Baldwin\'s design, at the junction of the Old Main Line and the new Metropolitan Branch to Washington. CSX used it for maintenance crews until 2022, when its windows were boarded up. The south awning fell under the snow of 2010 and was rebuilt from 2011.',
  'A red brick, triangular station with a four-storey tower under a pyramidal roof, dormers and a cupola. It is still a MARC commuter stop, but the building itself is closed.',
  'Baldwin\'s triangle-shaped station at the railroad fork.',
  accn='Railroad property (CSX); the platforms serve MARC trains. See it from the MARC platforms or the road; do not cross the tracks. Commuter parking was not checked.',
  links=[W('Point of Rocks station')],brick='yes'),
N('st-pauls-church-point-of-rocks','St. Paul\'s Episcopal Church','Point of Rocks','md','civic','standing',39.28715,-77.52894,'exact','view','drive','','1842',
  'A small late Federal church built in 1842 of local brick by enslaved people from the nearby Duvall plantation, and consecrated in 1843. Union troops used it as a hospital and stripped its furnishings; the parish later won $1,000 in the Court of Claims for repairs. It fell to twice-yearly use after a new chapel was built, and the congregation came back to it in 1966.',
  'An austere brick church with a Dutch gable and a Palladian window. Interior not checked.',
  'Built by enslaved hands, stripped by the Union army.',
  accn='An active Episcopal parish on Catoctin Mountain Highway (US 15), with its own small lot. Seen from outside; service times and interior not checked.',
  links=[W('St. Paul\'s Episcopal Church (Point of Rocks, Maryland)')],
  th=[39.28782,-77.52940],thn='Church lot off US 15',brick='yes'),
# ---- South Mountain ----
N('gathland-state-park','Gathland: War Correspondents Arch and Townsend ruins','Burkittsville','md','landmark','ruins',39.40575,-77.63926,'site','public','walk','','1884–1896',
  'The mountain estate of George Alfred Townsend, the Civil War correspondent who signed himself "Gath", in Crampton\'s Gap, where part of the Battle of South Mountain was fought in 1862. He bought the land in 1884 and built Gapland Hall and a stone lodge in 1885, a large den and library building in 1890, a mausoleum in 1895 and in 1896 the War Correspondents Memorial Arch, the first monument to journalists killed in war. The state took the estate in 1949.',
  'The arch beside the Appalachian Trail; the hall and lodge, partly restored in the 1950s and now museums; the foundation and fragments of the den and library building; and the shell of the unused mausoleum, which lost its bronze dog, with "Good Night Gath" on its marble lintel (Wikipedia, DNR).',
  'A reporter\'s castle on a battlefield, with an empty tomb.',
  accn='Gathland State Park (Maryland DNR), 900 Arnoldstown Road, Jefferson. Open 8 a.m. to sunset April to September, 10 a.m. to sunset October to March (DNR). The arch is maintained by the National Park Service. Museum hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Gathland State Park'),['Maryland DNR: Gathland State Park',DNR+'gathland.aspx']],
  th=[39.40627,-77.63907],thn='Gathland lot, Arnoldtown Road',brick='?'),
N('washington-monument-boonsboro','Washington Monument, South Mountain','Boonsboro','md','landmark','standing',39.50048,-77.62331,'exact','public','hike','','1827; rebuilt 1936',
  'A dry-laid stone tower that the people of Boonsboro built on Monument Knob on July 4, 1827, the first finished monument to George Washington; they came back in September to bring it to 30 feet. Union signalmen used it in the Civil War. Odd Fellows restored it in 1882, but it was neglected, dynamited at least once and by 1916 was half its height. The Civilian Conservation Corps rebuilt it and it was rededicated on July 4, 1936.',
  'The CCC\'s rebuilt round stone tower on the knob, on the Appalachian Trail, with a museum at the park about the monument and the South Mountain battle.',
  'The first Washington Monument, built by a town in a day.',
  accn='Washington Monument State Park (Maryland DNR), 6620 Zittlestown Road, Middletown. Open 8 a.m. to sunset April to September, 10 a.m. to sunset October to March (DNR). A walk uphill from the main lot.',
  links=[W('Washington Monument State Park'),['Maryland DNR: Washington Monument State Park',DNR+'washington.aspx'],['Maryland DNR: monument history',DNR+'Washington/History-Washington.aspx']],
  th=[39.49900,-77.62379],thn='Washington Monument State Park lot',brick='no'),
]
